#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293780 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb30dbbf-4f83-59d0-b712-f0e592aafb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Curved Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c18aa5b7-c992-5b32-a357-5e724ba96bd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9+\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293782}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c18aa5b7-c992-5b32-a357-5e724ba96bd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9+\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293782} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4170770-80a0-5c01-a8a3-394cfa36e747" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293781}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f14565cc-b995-5571-b40e-c5bb6b6d3c35" :question/math-academy-id "q-316261" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065207/assets/d36f2859865da58d226a738d8338f726df950db75b7421b604bb692c9049d54a.png)\n\nIf the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above, then\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [-4,-2]$ and the line segment for $x\\in (-2,1].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-2}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,-2].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot (1)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-2,1].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2+1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi +9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349678 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4abe11e-febf-5461-96ee-bca8a7713bdf" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Linear Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a028ffc-68fc-56aa-9e1f-12f2e817955e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349680} {:answer/id #uuid "611f6b0e-9f94-5fa2-9203-2bb6b690e6da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "611f6b0e-9f94-5fa2-9203-2bb6b690e6da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349681} :answer-field/id #uuid "d125695a-c155-5239-9b5f-5b6e05520a60" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349679}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3010a5d1-7b47-53cc-a84b-d481e0b19c2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-338331" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065207/assets/06ace831b877406e48fe8f1ab38a7244e7ade878d7c77b99d8230f40ad307207.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of four line segments. Then,\n\n${\\int }_{-6}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of four parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-6,-3],$ the line segment for $x\\in (-3,0],$ the line segment for $x\\in (0,2],$ and the line segment for $x\\in (2,5].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-6}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-6}^{-3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}+{{{\\int }_{2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{4}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-6,-3].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}3(3) \\\\  & =4.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-3,0].$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =bh \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (0,2].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4+2)(2) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $4$th integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (2,5].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}3(6) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-6}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}+{A}_{3}+{A}_{4} \\\\  & =4.5+3+6+9 \\\\  & =22.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349682 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4abe11e-febf-5461-96ee-bca8a7713bdf" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Linear Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68a8cc72-ed57-55c6-b912-bef9bcbc34b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68a8cc72-ed57-55c6-b912-bef9bcbc34b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349684} :answer-field/id #uuid "47bd312d-0b86-53fa-8d24-e7da5c89a0ee" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349683}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a967fa2f-cf50-596f-b417-99d097139ba9" :question/math-academy-id "q-338336" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065207/assets/19b94b149f752dc284f2cc002d786bc002621333a8eaadf2396e131640046c89.png)\n\nThe graph $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Then,\n\n${\\int }_{-5}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We divide the interval of integration into $3$ regions: the line segment for $x\\in [-5,0],$ the line segment for $x\\in (0,3],$ and the line segment for $x\\in [3,5].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-5}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{3}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-5,0].$   The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+4)(5) \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (0,3].$    The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3+0)(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [3,5].$   The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(0+2)(2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{3}=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}-{A}_{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{2}+\\frac{9}{2}-2 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349685 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb30dbbf-4f83-59d0-b712-f0e592aafb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Curved Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06c9540c-cff1-5224-9d33-42957263b6dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18+\\frac{9\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349687}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06c9540c-cff1-5224-9d33-42957263b6dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18+\\frac{9\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349687} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddd8f320-8e7b-54fd-956a-5b186a08dbb7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "98021bae-338d-5362-9995-8e98cc8d72d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-339266" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065207/assets/f418496b3460fddc13ea2beb4c099e97ed4d2fa1d89e04c01862d142e4d4177f.png)\n\nIf the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above, then\n\n${\\int }_{-6}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of $3$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-6,-3),$ the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in (-3,3),$ and the line segment for $x\\in (3,6].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-6}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-6}^{-3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-6,-3).$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4+1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-3,3).$ The corresponding region is a combination of a semi-circle of radius $3$ and a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =bh+\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =6(1)+\\frac{1}{2}\\pi (3)^{2} \\\\  & =6+\\frac{9\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (3,6].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+2)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-6}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}+6+\\frac{9\\pi }{2}+\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =18+\\frac{9\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078946 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb30dbbf-4f83-59d0-b712-f0e592aafb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Curved Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7304737-cba6-5fbc-b8be-0f1ac860d786" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10 + \\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076888} {:answer/id #uuid "27e19e5e-9ae9-55e3-a6b1-2d3041c09690" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5 + π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076889} {:answer/id #uuid "2aa18fed-eec9-5887-a749-9f71fb14a6c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10 + π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076890} {:answer/id #uuid "2f8ad594-e424-5b7f-9948-5e8da6618757" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5 + \\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076891} {:answer/id #uuid "33d74abb-4ac1-5932-857d-5df1fe3e33ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10 + \\frac{3π}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076892}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7304737-cba6-5fbc-b8be-0f1ac860d786" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10 + \\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076888} :answer-field/id #uuid "6463ff6a-e667-5122-8f6a-66c6d265d077" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076887}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b82eafa4-95ed-5313-a838-9f9ba69b6d8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-49176" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065207/assets/83b23d8138091a931d14854b197752d25d8149ee8914a70b8db73b6c2369c888.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n${\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-2,3)$ and the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [3,5].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,3).$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =b\\cdot h \\\\  & =5\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [3,5].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot (1)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =10+\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
